  "dom_id": "step-q182718",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "If $S(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}},$ then $S(k+1)=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to express the summation $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$\n\nLet ${a}_{i}$ for $i\\ge 0$ be a sequence, and let $S(k)$ denote the sum of the first $k+1$ terms.\n\n$$\nS(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i}\n$$\n\nWe can always express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k)$ by writing the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${a}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+(\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}),\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as \n\n$$\nS(k+1)=S(k)+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}.\n$$",
